      "v": 7,
      "zh": "他们用手持住你，你就断折，伤了他们的肩；他们倚靠你，你就断折，闪了他们的腰。",
      "en": "when they grasped you with their hand,[NOTE:1] you broke and tore[NOTE:2] their shoulders,and when they leaned on you, you splintered and caused their legs to be unsteady.[NOTE:3]",
      "notes": [
        "The Hebrew consonantal text (Kethib) implies בְכַפְּךָ (vekappeka, “by your hand”) but the marginal reading (Qere) has simply בַכַּף (vakkaf, “by the hand”). The LXX reads: “with their hand,” implying בְכַפָּם (vekappam).",
        "Or perhaps “dislocated.”",
        "Heb “you caused to stand for them all their hips.” An emendation that switches two letters but is supported by the LXX yields the reading: “you caused all their hips to shake.” See L. C. Allen, Ezekiel (WBC), 2:103. In 2 Kgs 18:21 and Isa 36:6 trusting in the Pharaoh is compared to leaning on a staff. The oracle may reflect Hophra’s attempt to aid Jerusalem (Jer 37:5-8)."
      ],
